hevc_rpivid: the HEVC decoder with the Raspberry Pi 4 rpivid block as an hwaccel

A second FFCodec, "hevc_rpivid", is FFmpeg's HEVC decoder with its new "rpivid"
option on (1: the default tool set; 0 off, 2 all tools; -1 = 1 for hevc_rpivid,
0 for hevc; FFMPEG_RPIVID=0|1|2 sets the default of hevc_rpivid). It is listed
before "hevc" in allcodecs.c, so avcodec_find_decoder(AV_CODEC_ID_HEVC) returns it.

With the option on, get_format() -- after a software format was chosen -- attaches
ff_hevc_rpivid_hwaccel (libavcodec/rpivid_hevc.c, BSD-3-Clause, from the port's
files/rpivid/src): an hwaccel that decodes into the system-memory frames the decoder
allocated, so players see a software decoder. A stream the block cannot take stays
on the CPU; a picture it cannot take, or a picture the block fails, switches the
rest of the stream to the CPU. The CPU decoder lacks the block-decoded pictures'
motion fields (TMVP), so it starts at the next IRAP: the pictures up to it (and the
RASL pictures of a CRA) are dropped, and the caller keeps showing its last picture.
At that IRAP the block is taken again (at most FFMPEG_RPIVID_RETRIES times per stream,
default 3; not under frame threads), so one refused or failed picture costs one GOP.

The option "rpivid_out" (FFMPEG_RPIVID_OUT): planar (the default, as above) or
drm_prime: get_format() then answers AV_PIX_FMT_DRM_PRIME and the hwaccel allocates
the frames itself (the block's GPU-importable buffers, libavcodec/rpivid_drm.h,
installed); on a fallback the pictures held move to system memory first
(ff_rpivid_hevc_drm_to_cpu), and the SEI picture hash is not checked on DRM frames.

  configure        hevc_rpivid_decoder: deps pthreads, selects hevc_decoder
  Makefile         the rpivid objects, rpivid_drm.h installed
  allcodecs.c      ff_hevc_rpivid_decoder before ff_hevc_decoder
  decode.c         per-frame hwaccel data without a frames context
  hevcdec.[ch]     the options, the attach/fallback points, SAO buffers kept for a
                   fallback, the SEI picture hash also checked on block-decoded
                   frames, the hevc_rpivid FFCodec

